cStreams consist of both Intermediate and Terminal operations

Intermediate Operations: Operations like map(), filter(), which return a stream.
Terminal Operation: Ends the stream pipeline and produces a result, like collect() or forEach().
Intermediate operations only execute when a terminal operation is invoked. Without a terminal operation, the stream is created but remains unprocessed.

Examples of return types:
Symbol: T
Meaning: Input type(Element type) of the stream before mapping

Symbol: R
Meaning: Result type(Type of stream after mapping)

Conventionally: You can use, T(Type), R(Result), K(Key), and V(Value).

Examples of Intermediate Operations

filter(Predicate): Filters elements based on a given condition.  return type: Stream<T>
map(function): Transforms elements by applying a function to each element. Return type: Stream<R>
flatMap(Function): Flattens and transforms elements by applying a function that returns a stream.  Stream<R>
distinct(): Returns a stream with only unique elements (removes duplicates).  Stream<T>
sorted()  : Sorts the elements in natural order or with a comparator. Stream<T>
limit(long n): Truncates the stream to the first n elements.  Stream<T>
skip(long n) : Skips the first  n elements of the stream.  Stream<T>
peek(Consumer): Applies a Consumer to each element, useful for debugging.  Stream<T>

Examples of Terminal Operations

collect(Collectors): Collects the stream elements into a collection or other data structures. Return type: Depends on collector
forEach(Consumer): Applies a Consumer to each element of the stream.Return type: void
reduce(BinaryOperator): Reduces the elements into a single value using an accumulator function. Optional<T> or T
count(): Counts the number of elements in the stream. return type: long
findFirst(): Returns the first element, if present. Optional<T>
findAny(): Returns any element, which is useful in parallel streams. Optional<T>
allMatch(Predicate): Checks if all elements match the given predicate. return type: boolean
anyMatch(Predicate): Checks if any element matches the given predicate. boolean
noneMatch (Predicate): Checks if no elements match the given predicate. boolean



